        "math_academy_id": "q-83185",
        "problem": "Find the first four non-zero terms in the Taylor expansion of $\\frac{1}{x+3}$ about the point $x=-2.$",
        "worked_solution": "We use Taylor's theorem in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{\u2032}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{\u2033}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+\\frac{{f}^{\u2034}(a)}{3!}(x-a)^{3}+\u22ef,\n$$\n\nwith $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+3}$ and $a=-2.$ \n\nComputing the required derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{1}{x+3}=(x+3)^{-1} \\\\ f(-2) & =(-2+3)^{-1}=1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{(x+3)^{2}}=-(x+3)^{-2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(-2) & =-(-2+3)^{-2}=-1 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{2}{(x+3)^{3}}=2(x+3)^{-3} \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(-2) & =2(-2+3)^{-3}=2 \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(x) & =-\\frac{6}{(x+3)^{4}}=-6(x+3)^{-4} \\\\ {f}^{\u2034}(-2) & =-6(-2+3)^{-4}=-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x+3} & =1+(-1)(x+2)+\\frac{2}{2!}(x+2)^{2}+\\frac{(-6)}{3!}(x+2)^{3}+\u22ef \\\\  & =1-(x+2)+(x+2)^{2}-(x+2)^{3}+\u22ef.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "actual_result": "Correct",
